<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Conwy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Afon Conwy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Conwy is a river in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, about 55 km long, that rises in the uplands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snowdonia">Snowdonia</a> and flows north through the Conwy valley to the sea at the town of Conwy. It long formed a frontier between Gwynedd west of the river and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perfeddwlad">Perfeddwlad</a> to the east, including the kingdom of Rhos. Edward I built Conwy Castle at its mouth in the 1280s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-conwy.jpg" alt="River Conwy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dbenbenn · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boats_in_River_Conwy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Conwy is 34 miles (55 km) long from its source to Conwy Bay and drains an area of 262 square miles (678 km²). Its name comes from the old Welsh words cyn, 'chief', and gwy, 'water', and the river was originally the Cynwy; it was formerly anglicised as Conway. It rises on the Migneint moor, where small streams feed Llyn Conwy, and flows north, joined by the Machno and the Lledr before Betws-y-coed, where the Llugwy joins it. It then passes Llanrwst, Trefriw and Dolgarrog. At spring tides it is tidal as far as Tan-lan, near Llanrwst.</p><p>The river has two different banks. Silurian mudstone hills lie to the east, covered in moor and, in places, planted conifers. Harder Cambrian rocks lie to the west, where the river falls in cascades and waterfalls at Conwy Falls and in the Lledr Gorge. Copper, lead and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> have been mined in the valley since Roman times. Below Betws-y-coed the valley is wide and fertile and supports dairying and sheep, and in winter the pastures take sheep brought down from the mountains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>There is evidence of settlement in the valley back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> occupied the area until about 400 CE. The churches of Llanrhychwyn and Llangelynnin, among the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, date from the 11th and 12th centuries. In 1466, after a Lancastrian attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbigh">Denbigh</a>, the Earl of Pembroke laid waste much of the valley during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>, on the orders of the Yorkist king Edward IV.</p><p>Deganwy Castle, on the estuary, was the original defensive position of the area. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, destroyed it in 1263, and its problems of resupply in a siege led to a new castle being built across the water at the town of Conwy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Conwy Castle, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, stands at the river's mouth, beside two well-known bridges: Thomas Telford's early suspension bridge, now a footpath, and Robert Stephenson's tubular iron bridge, which still carries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holyhead">Holyhead</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> railway line. A third bridge carries road traffic, and the A55 passes under the estuary in the Conwy Crossing, an immersed tube tunnel opened in October 1991. Aber Afon Conwy is a site of special scientific interest, and the river is noted for salmon and sea trout.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Conwy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Edward I conquered Wales in 1277–1283 and secured it with castles such as Caernarfon, <b>Conwy</b> and Harlech, but his attempt to subdue Scotland failed, and his son Edward II was defeated at the Battle of Bannockburn in 1314.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Rhodri the Great killed the Danish leader Horm in 856, and in 881 his son Anarawd defeated the Mercians at <b>Conwy</b>, a victory the annals called vengeance for Rhodri.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Rhos lay between the <b>River Conwy</b> and the Clwydian hills, on the coast of modern Conwy county.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-rhos">Kingdom of Rhos</a></li></ul></section>
